The backfill logic for Db2 has been altered to use a SELECT that returns tuples, but the subsequent code expects dictionary rows and accesses fields via .get(). This mismatch will cause runtime errors. The test harness also relies on the cursor providing a description, which is not set in the new implementation. The changes should be reverted or corrected to use a proper fetchall that returns dicts or adjust the code to handle tuples.
  • (high) Backfill cursor returns tuples but code expects dicts [mnemos/persistence/db2.py:2020-2035]
      In Db2MemoryRepository.backfill_missing_content_hashes the SELECT statement now returns "id, content" tuples. The code then calls _fetch_all_dicts(cursor) and accesses row.get("content"), assuming each row is a dict. Since the cursor returns tuples and has no description, _fetch_all_dicts will fail or return empty, and row.get will raise AttributeError. This will break the backfill operation.
  next:
    - Revert the SELECT to the original form that returns dict rows, or modify the code to use cursor.fetchall() and index into the tuple (row[1]) instead of row.get("content"). Ensure the cursor has a description if _fetch_all_dicts is used.

